---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "usage", label: "Usage" },
  { id: "horizontal", label: "Horizontal scrolling" },
  { id: "page", label: "Page scrollbar" },
  { id: "container", label: "Custom container bar" },
  { id: "styling", label: "Custom styling" },
]);
const tags = Array.from({ length: 50 }, (_, index) => `v1.2.0-beta.${50 - index}`);
---
<DocsLayout page="scroll-area" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.usage}>
    <p>Add <code>.scroll-area</code> to a container with a bounded height or width.
      It applies <code>overflow: auto</code>, a thin scrollbar, and theme-aware colors.
      Wheel, touch, keyboard, and scrollbar dragging remain native. It needs no JavaScript initialization.</p>
    <div class="tags-demo scroll-area p-4" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="tags-title">
      <h2 id="tags-title" class="title-small mt-0 mb-2">Tags</h2>
      <ul class="tag-list">
        {tags.map(tag => <li class="py-2">{tag}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <Code code={`<div class="scroll-area p-4" style="width: 12rem; height: 18rem"
  tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="release-tags-title">
  <h2 id="release-tags-title" class="title-small mt-0">Tags</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>v1.2.0-beta.50</li>
    <li>v1.2.0-beta.49</li>
    <!-- More tags -->
  </ul>
</div>`} />
    <p>Give a generic scroll region <code>tabindex="0"</code> and an accessible name
      so keyboard users can focus and scroll it. Keep padding for child focus rings.
      In a flex or grid layout, add <code>min-h-0</code> or <code>min-w-0</code>
      when the scroll container needs to shrink.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.horizontal}>
    <p>The same class scrolls horizontally when content exceeds the container's width.</p>
    <div class="scroll-area p-2" style="max-width: 24rem" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Release channels">
      <div class="inline-flex gap-3 text-nowrap">
        {['Stable releases', 'Beta releases', 'Nightly builds', 'Archived releases'].map(label => <span class="surface-container p-4">{label}</span>)}
      </div>
    </div>
    <Code code={`<div class="scroll-area p-2" style="max-width: 24rem"
  tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Release channels">
  <div class="inline-flex gap-3 text-nowrap">
    <span class="surface-container p-4">Stable releases</span>
    <span class="surface-container p-4">Beta releases</span>
    <span class="surface-container p-4">Nightly builds</span>
    <span class="surface-container p-4">Archived releases</span>
  </div>
</div>`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.page}>
    <p>Add the class to <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> to style the page's own scrollbar.
      The viewport keeps native scrolling, and the thumb color follows the theme.</p>
    <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="scroll-area">`} />
    <h3>Custom page bar</h3>
    <p>Opt in with <code>&lt;html class="scroll-area custom"&gt;</code> and initialize
      <code>ScrollArea</code>. This modifier draws a vertical bar at the
      inline-end edge. The generated bar is hidden
      from assistive technology and takes pointer input. Wheel, keyboard, touch
      and fragment scrolling remain native.</p>
    <p>Drag the thumb to move through the page. Press the track above or below it
      to scroll one viewport in that direction. Holding the press does not repeat.
      A primary press keeps focus and the selection. Dragging stays instant even on a page
      with smooth scrolling; a track press follows the page's scroll behavior.</p>
    <button type="button" id="page-bar-demo" aria-pressed="false">Show custom page bar</button>
    <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="scroll-area custom">`} />
    <Code code={`// Call after the body exists, or use Expressive.AutoInit().
const area = Expressive.ScrollArea.init(document.documentElement);
area.refresh(); // After content overflows a fixed-height body or a custom scroll lock changes.
area.destroy(); // Remove the drawn bar and restore the native one.`} />
    <p><code>ScrollArea</code> has no options or events. <code>AutoInit()</code> and
      <code>AutoInit(document.body, options)</code> claim the matching
      <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> unless it has <code>.no-autoinit</code>.
      Calling <code>init()</code> twice replaces the earlier instance.</p>
    <p>The thumb follows the document's height with a 48px minimum. A scroll timeline
      drives its position where supported; a passive scroll listener does so elsewhere.
      Reduced motion keeps the position accurate. Size changes update automatically;
      call <code>refresh()</code> when content overflows a fixed-height body, after
      changing your own scroll lock, or after adding a body to an instance started before it existed.</p>
    <p>Without JavaScript, or after removing <code>.custom</code>, the themed native
      bar remains. Forced colors and a coarse primary pointer use the native bar.
      Print hides the drawn bar. A modal dialog hides it while open. On systems with
      classic scrollbars, hiding the native bar widens the page once at initialization.</p>
    <p>The default 16px inset of <code>.back-to-top</code> and <code>.fab-menu</code>
      becomes 18px beside the custom bar. The page-level app bar's trailing padding
      also becomes 18px, leaving 6px between each control and the 12px bar.
      <code>--scroll-area-bar-clearance</code> grows with wider bars and preserves
      larger author offsets. It stays set while the instance exists, including
      during a modal lock or when the page cannot scroll, to avoid shifting controls.</p>
    <p>For your own edge control, use
      <code>inset-inline-end: max(16px, var(--scroll-area-bar-clearance, 0px))</code>.
      Set <code>--scroll-area-bar-size</code> and <code>dir</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>.
      Give <code>--md-comp-back-to-top-offset</code> and <code>--md-comp-fab-menu-offset</code>
      a length with a unit beside the custom bar. The bar takes presses over edge
      content, so keep interactive content clear and choose a contrasting thumb color.</p>
    <p>Fixed <code>.back-to-top</code> and <code>.fab-menu</code> controls inside a
      shadow root also read the document's inherited clearance. Automatic app-bar
      padding applies only to document-level headers. For a viewport-wide app bar
      inside a shadow root, add
      <code>padding-inline-end: var(--scroll-area-bar-clearance, 4px)</code>
      to its bar element in that root's stylesheet.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.container}>
    <div id="container-bar-demo" class="scroll-area custom" style="block-size: 300px; inline-size: 240px" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Custom scroll area example">
      <div style="block-size: 900px; padding: 16px 18px 16px 16px">Scroll this 900px content with a proportional thumb.</div>
    </div>
    <Code code={`<div class="scroll-area custom" style="block-size: 300px; inline-size: 240px"
  tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Release tags">
  <div style="block-size: 900px">Release tags</div>
</div>`} />
    <Code code={`const area = Expressive.ScrollArea.init(document.querySelector('.scroll-area.custom'));
area.refresh();
area.destroy();`} />
    <p>For a custom container bar, add <code>.scroll-area.custom</code> to a bounded container and initialize it with <code>Expressive.ScrollArea.init(el)</code> or <code>AutoInit()</code>. A generated <code>.scroll-area-wrapper</code> with <code>display: contents !important</code> holds the container and its bar. Do not author that wrapper, bar or thumb. <code>destroy()</code> removes the bar and returns the container to its parent. Keep the region's authored role, name and keyboard focusability.</p>
    <p>A 300px-tall container with 900px of content has a 100px thumb. Dragging moves only that container; a track press scrolls one container height, once. Primary presses preserve focus and selection and cancel the generated click, so ancestor links and bubbling click handlers do not activate. Content clicks and secondary-button behavior stay native. The container's thumb uses a passive, frame-coalesced scroll listener in every engine. Its element children and container size are observed; direct child additions and removals also trigger a measurement. Attribute changes on the container or its composed ancestors and system color-scheme or contrast changes queue a measurement. <code>refresh()</code> remeasures after direct text-node changes or stylesheet changes that resize nothing. Each measurement reads the container's computed direction, borders, <code>color</code>, <code>color-scheme</code>, <code>z-index</code>, visibility, <code>--scroll-area-bar-size</code>, <code>--scroll-area-thumb-color</code> and <code>--scroll-area-track-color</code>. Ancestor values apply; removing a container override restores them. The bar follows the container's inline-end padding edge, inside its border, with the container's stacking value. It hides when vertical scrolling is unavailable or the container is not visible or rendered. A closed dialog is measured again when it opens.</p>
    <p>The native bar returns without an instance or <code>.custom</code>, under forced colors, with a coarse primary pointer, in print, or when the vertical drawn bar is hidden. Container bars set no page clearance. A container inside a modal dialog works; opening a modal outside it, adding <code>inert</code>, removing <code>.custom</code>, or changing the bar's media conditions ends a drag until the next press. A modal in a separate inaccessible closed shadow root cannot cancel a drag if it exposes no external focus transition. Its owner must destroy affected <code>ScrollArea</code> instances before <code>showModal()</code> and reinitialize them after closing; <code>refresh()</code> cannot detect or cancel this case. With AutoInit watching, opt those containers out before destroying them. Multiple, nested, iframe and shadow-root instances keep separate targets and anchor names.</p>
    <p>Container limits:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The generated <code>.scroll-area-wrapper</code> has no box and replaces the container among its parent's children. Child and sibling selectors, <code>:first-child</code>, <code>:nth-child</code>, <code>::slotted()</code> and scripts reading its parent see the wrapper. Put a container inside a scaffold pane, dialog body or pane body, rather than on that body. The semantics checker forbids other parents whose content model or direct-child styling cannot accept the wrapper.</li>
      <li><code>init</code> and <code>destroy()</code> move the container. An iframe inside reloads, animations restart, native audio/video keeps its play state, custom elements disconnect and reconnect, open dialogs or popovers inside close, nested scrollers may reset and text selections may collapse. Focus returns to the container, its light-DOM content and reachable open-shadow descendants. A container inside a closed shadow root is supported. A closed root inside the container hides its focused leaf, so its owner must restore focus afterward or move focus outside that descendant before the call. Calling <code>AutoInit()</code> again moves each container twice. Under <code>watch: true</code>, destroying a still-matching container starts it again; remove <code>.custom</code> or add <code>.no-autoinit</code> first to keep it destroyed.</li>
      <li>A rendering library that owns the parent must call <code>destroy()</code> before moving or removing the container.</li>
      <li>A scrolling ancestor clips the bar only if it, or an intervening ancestor, has a <code>position</code>. Use <code>position: relative</code> on an author's scroller. The scaffold's pane, dialog body and pane body provide it while they hold a wrapper.</li>
      <li>Positive <code>z-index</code> content can cover the bar. Add <code>isolation: isolate</code> on the container when its content must stack below it.</li>
      <li>Put <code>opacity</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>transform</code> and animations on a parent so they also affect the sibling bar. Attribute changes remeasure on the next frame. Call <code>refresh()</code> after editing stylesheet rules for container <code>visibility</code>, <code>dir</code>, <code>color</code>, <code>color-scheme</code>, <code>z-index</code> or scroll-area properties when no resize or observed attribute change triggers a measurement.</li>
      <li>The default bar covers the last 12px. Give controls at least 24px by 24px and 18px of inline-end padding. Wider bars need more padding. For smaller controls, check the <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html">WCAG 2.5.8 spacing rule and exceptions</a>; 18px alone may not separate their target circles.</li>
      <li>A wheel over the bar forwards pixel deltas, uses 40px per line or one container height per page, and scrolls instantly without smooth wheel animation. It chains at the end even with <code>overscroll-behavior: contain</code>. Ctrl-wheel remains native zoom.</li>
      <li>An engine without anchor positioning keeps the native bar.</li>
      <li>A <code>position: fixed</code> container is unsupported. The bar follows a sticky container during parent scrolling.</li>
      <li>While the vertical bar is drawn, the horizontal native bar is hidden too. A sideways-only scroller keeps its native bar; wheel, keys and touch still scroll sideways.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.styling}>
    <p>Set these properties on the scroll container or an ancestor.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--scroll-area-thumb-color</code></td><td><code>var(--md-sys-color-on-surface-variant)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scroll-area-track-color</code></td><td><code>transparent</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scroll-area-bar-size</code></td><td><code>12px</code>, custom drawn bars</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scroll-area-bar-clearance</code></td><td><code>18px</code> with the default custom page bar, grows with its width</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--expressive-z-scroll-area</code></td><td><code>999</code> for the page bar, above app bars and below non-modal sheets</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <Code code={`.release-tags {
  --scroll-area-thumb-color: var(--md-sys-color-primary);
  --scroll-area-track-color: var(--md-sys-color-surface-container);
}`} />
    <p>Use colors with enough contrast against the track and container.
      <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-scrollbars-1/">Native scrollbar styling</a>
      follows the browser and operating system, so shape, exact width, and automatic
      hiding can differ. Older WebKit engines use rounded 8px scrollbars.
      Forced-colors mode restores system colors and the default width.</p>
    <p>For a selective Sass build, include <code>"scroll-area"</code> in <code>$utilities</code>.</p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    const button = document.getElementById('page-bar-demo');
    button.addEventListener('click', function () {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const active = button.getAttribute('aria-pressed') !== 'true';
      root.classList.toggle('scroll-area', active);
      root.classList.toggle('custom', active);
      if (active) Expressive.ScrollArea.init(root);
      else Expressive.ScrollArea.getInstance(root)?.destroy();
      button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(active));
      button.textContent = active ? 'Hide custom page bar' : 'Show custom page bar';
    });
  });
</script>

<style>
  .tags-demo {
    width: 12rem;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: 18rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--md-sys-color-outline-variant);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
  .tag-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .tag-list > li + li { border-top: 1px solid var(--md-sys-color-outline-variant); }
</style>
